#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:5
#: 461d4e6feff04ba7903f1e15f0b9f7a2
msgid "Sonar Sensors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:7
#: d2940d14eeac4680800a8094e69be264
msgid "**ARCHIVED**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:9
#: 723b43b0207946cda0aeb7f679d08538
msgid "Sonar sensors can provide obstacle avoidance functionality for Rover. This page provides instructions for installing, configuring, and testing single and double sonar setups."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:15
#: 6914fd963d5246f2b106c752513d568d
msgid "This article is out of date. Sonars are now more generically represented by \"Rangefinders\" - Rover can support 4. The relevant parameters have morphed from \"SONAR\" to \"RNGFND\" . See the ``RNGFNDx_y`` parameters, such as :ref:`RNGFND1_TYPE<RNGFND1_TYPE>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:20
#: 08f44f0aa08f48459851f8e052e32bcb
msgid "What you'll need"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:22
#: fc2780c879d044e598973abab80208d0
msgid "Sonar (ultrasonic) sensors allow Rover to detect obstacles and avoid them. Sonar sensors can be more sensitive than IR sensors, making them the preferred option for obstacle avoidance. Normally, a single sonar sensor is used, at the front of the rover. But Rover also supports the use of two sonar sensors, one pointing a bit to the right and the other a bit to the left, to not only detect obstacles but steer away from them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:30
#: 423f504e42814a41a444cd9ea8ede202
msgid "To implement sonar sensors, you will need:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:32
#: 6561125e05ef46d3a8a9f7535332f6fa
msgid "One or two `Maxbotics MB1240 XL Maxsonar EZ4 sensors <http://www.maxbotix.com/Ultrasonic_Sensors/MB1240.htm>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:33
#: d85b8bcad0e94410b8aba0b6361965d2
msgid "A servo extension cable with female connector on one end to connect each sensor (or cut one end off a `female-female servo cable <http://www.hobbytronics.co.uk/radio-control/servo-cables-connectors/servo-ext-cable-6in>`__)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:37
#: 33839be6c9b343a68aae13729f98bf0c
msgid "Connect the sensor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:39
#: ac4742f1aba64645bc78d4cfaa146aaa
msgid "Solder the servo cable wires to the sensor's GND (black wire), V+ (red wire) and \"3\" (white wire) holes as shown below. *Click on images to enlarge.*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:46
#: 040d65bd563a41979cce7c0297e5bc94
msgid "Then plug the servo connector into APM 2's A0 pins as shown in the picture below. If you're using a second sonar sensor, plug it into A1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:52
#: 8157289932d04365bbf43f473a49ad3d
msgid "Next, mount your sonar sensor on the front of your rover. To avoid interference from the ground, it should be either raised high off the ground and/or tilted slightly upwards. Below is an example, on a very small rover. The sonar sensor is on the front, nearest to us. The sensor on the other side is an IR sensor, used to compare results."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:61
#: cf768165522b4e5ab9a2eb83adf55662
msgid "**PX4 Sonar Pins:** Sonar is now supported for Rover on PX4FMU/PX4IO. You will need to assign one or both possible Sonars to the appropriate \"SONAR_PIN and SONAR2_PIN parameters in: Mission Planner - Configuration - Advanced Params - Adv Parameter List."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:66
#: 940339ae3c704f1ca5f2c73ae5449af6
msgid "The following PX4FMU/PX4IO \"Pins\" are available for Sonar use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:68
#: 064c0dc123e64dd0bbf29ee0fa2c53f8
msgid "PIN = 11 : The \"airspeed\" pin. Located on a 3 pin DF13 connector on the PX4IO board, but directly visible to the ADC on the PX4FMU. This pin can take voltages up to 6.6V (it has an internal voltage divider)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:72
#: 9e05a27a9e3649d2af17d99de17d1826
msgid "PIN = 12: A general analog input pin. Located on pin 3 of the \"FMUSPI\" port on the PX4IO board, this pin is directly visible to the PX4FMU analog input code. This can take voltages up to 3.3V."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:75
#: 523cca88016f42548aaf19d17b20594f
msgid "PIN = 13: A general analog input pin. Located on pin 4 of the \"FMUSPI\" port on the PX4IO board, this pin is directly visible to the PX4FMU analog input code. This can take voltages up to 3.3V.  It is being worked on and will be included in this section when it is available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:81
#: 377f6c45350c479fa99efb6f22cb3394
msgid "It is recommended to use the PX4IO board's FMU-SPI port connector pin 3 (SONAR_PIN = 12) and connector pin 4 (SONAR2_PIN = 13)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:85
#: a6baa66c1a3f4ce5b27db30860f73ee9
msgid "Enable sonar in Mission Planner"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:87
#: d0908088c43c4b07b0da1b166fe329b5
msgid "All configuration can be done via MAVLink parameters in the Mission Planner. There is no need to edit the Rover code itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:90
#: d69747ba64864e35960009fe46f3fc33
msgid "There are two ways to configure the sensors. You can either use the simplified interface in the \"Advanced Params\" option of the Mission Planner Configuration tab, or, if you want to get into more detailed control of the sensors, you can use the full set of options in the longer \"Adv Parameters List\". (Note: Although there is a sonar sensor enable option in the Hardware Options tab, this is just for Copter and Plane and is disabled for Rover. Because the Rover sonar settings are more detailed and have more options, they must be set in the Advanced lists)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:100
#: b9f0d5eb59124b3197abdf3540f02003
msgid "First, connect to Rover via USB or 3DR radio with the Mission Planner. Remember, select 115200 as the baud rate for USB or 57600 for the 3DR wireless radios."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:107
#: 561b757f4d6148bcb937fe5b5756214b
msgid "Once you've done that, go to the Configuration tab and select the Advanced Params option. Set \"Sonar enabled\" to enabled. (If you're using a second sonar sensor, we'll set that up in the next step.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:114
#: eb6b2edbdb9c440aaedf4e1a389f89c4
msgid "At this point, you should be able to see the sonar data in the Flight Data screen. Look for the little \"Tuning\" checkbox on the right side, under the map view, and check it. This will bring up the data window. Click on legend and this will bring up the \"Graph This\" window of data fields to display. Unselect everything but \"sonarrange\" and \"sonarvoltage\". At this point it should start displaying the real-time data from your primary sonar sensor, as shown below:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:126
#: 508f932116944a89bd182fa200327791
msgid "Enabling a second sonar sensor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:128
#: 8d6f813f3e8946fbb1a9c8be67079491
msgid "Having two sonar sensors tells APM:Rrover which way to turn to avoid an obstacle. If the obstacle is on the right, it turns left and vice versa. This is a little tricky to set up, but well worth it for reliable autonomy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:133
#: 4fd404ecf45b4f6181e8adc42620ca10
msgid "**Physical mounting:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:135
#: 5f0ef38827d7457f89a9c3ed2f50a4a5
msgid "Mount the two sensors facing about 10-15 degrees out from the front of the rover, and 10-15 degrees up from the horizontal, so they don't see the road/ground.  You should also mount them raised as high as you can, to avoid noise from ground reflections. In the picture below, they are raised as high as they can be and still comply with the 4\" height limit of the Sparkfun AVC competition Micro category rover."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:142
#: baafed7236c847efb5c1b9b0b8133b2c
msgid "If you're going to use aluminium mounts as shown below, make sure you have double-sided foam tape on the back of the sensors so they don't ground out on the metal mounts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:149
#: 6fdaaaab51914e78996e9c0ea823deba
msgid "Mount Sonar to Rover"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:151
#: e85b2fa028b04fab8b4d9b573ddef31a
msgid "The picture below shows twin sonar sensors mounted to Tom Coyle's Slash Rover (winner of the AVC 2013 Peloton Class). The shielded signal cable and capacitor (see section on power filtering) can be seen on the rover."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:158
#: be4b30ea428b4d0ebc15994d4e05dd21
msgid "**Electrical connections:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:160
#: a4c810162a4d4f13b25ac2b68c8e87a2
msgid "You can use any ports you want (A0-A8), but here's the way we do it: Connect the left sensor's connector to A0, and the right to A1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:163
#: 25b77f261054405a9bb96a96ac3bc62e
msgid "You're also going to need to connect a wire from each sensor's control pin to APM so it can tell the sensors when to read, so they don't both read at the same time and get echoes from each other's signals (this is explained in the Maxbotix datasheet `here <http://www.maxbotix.com/documents/XL-MaxSonar-EZ_Datasheet.pdf>`__). To do this, solder a wire from each sensor's pin #4 to a cable with a connector on the end that you can plug into APM, as shown below. We use standard `jumper cables <http://adafruit.com/products/266>`__ for this."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:175
#: 3ef5c7056a4a4c0d98ccc5d0789aff05
msgid "**Software setup:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:177
#: 87b34ff352f0493a804e34b2240cecd2
msgid "First, if you're using a second sonar sensor, you need to tell the software what APM pins it is connected to. You can do that in the Adv Parameters List in the \"SONAR2_PIN\" parameter (scroll down to the parameters that begin with SONAR). Typically you will set that to 1 and connect your second sonar sensor to APM 2.5's A1 port."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:183
#: 36aa6a79f5a04561a6f926f318643ee1
msgid "Likewise for the \"stop pins\" that APM uses to control when the sonar sensors fire, to ensure that they don't interfere with each other. We use A2 for Sonar 1 (left) and A3 for Sonar 2 (right)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:187
#: b1798fa788aa441da03f817677f3f4cc
msgid "The rest of the parameters shown below are appropriate for the recommended MB1240 sensors.  Here are a few other parameters you'll want to review and set as appropriate for your setup:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:191
#: e4e79950fa7349adbbba89dcd6dc60e4
msgid "SONAR_TRIGGER is the distance at which you want the sonar to start to cause a steering deviation when an object is detected. We typically use 300cm (3 meters)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:194
#: 38b12e389aaf472d82765dc2434ca06f
msgid "SONAR_SCALING is the value used to convert the input voltage into distance. Since we use the MB1240 sonar, our scaling parameter is 2.04 which is based on 4.9mv/cm @5vdc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:197
#: 649d24be190e463d8799dad9c804f1aa
msgid "SONAR_TURN_ANGLE is the angle, in degrees, the steering will make when the trigger distance is detected. A positive value will cause a right turn and a negative value will cause a left turn when only one sonar is in use.  We typically use 25 degrees for that."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:201
#: 0fd3a874522049adb179b6f164ab3ff2
msgid "SONAR_TURN_TIME is the amount of time that the steering will deviate when the trigger distance is detected. Defaults to 1 sec. We've actually used a smaller value (0.2 seconds) in the below so it doesn't turn away as long (we just want it to veer, not turn around)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:206
#: 62ffb2c6cbbe427e8f36f1cd5d37aeb7
msgid "All of these parameters, along with the others not documented here, are fully described in :doc:`the Parameters List here <parameters>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:213
#: b3d08d313dc54d878e9c3ff8fdb82e0c
msgid "Testing your sonar setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:215
#: a31872e1a9974eaa9d363ca8841ca084
msgid "The best way to test your sonar is by driving your rover around in Steering mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:218
#: 310c47cf095947a9bc5fda9d512a46db
msgid "**Steering Mode:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:220
#: 18c14795925142bc89b24ce30dcd1a66
msgid "The best way to test both your settings and the physical placement of your sensors is in \"Steering Mode\", which will let you drive around in manual RC mode but will override you when the sensors detect an obstacle. Either assign that mode to a position on your RC Mode Switch or select it via the Mission Planner over the wireless telemetry link by using the Actions box on the Mission Planner Flight Data screen as shown below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:231
#: 729d01425c614f3c87521892786d710a
msgid "When you're in this mode, approach obstacles and ensure that the rover steers away as you expect. A few troubleshooting tips:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:234
#: 84fdadfd730a42418e63ebaf51d5484a
msgid "If the rover detects phantom obstacles while just moving on open ground, try one or more of the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:237
#: 4ee788ea090b43ab8cac528dcfd8072c
msgid "Tilt the sensors up a bit more so they don't see the ground"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:238
#: 5b51257fadef46dbac3d83d5b244e823
msgid "Raise the SONAR_DEBOUNCE parameter from the default of 2 (50hz detections)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:240
#: 238fc15cf0a049f7a29a183c66c500cc
msgid "Turn the detection range a bit down from 300 cm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:243
#: 56ba53e6095244768054b96136c01aff
msgid "Power filtering (optional)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:245
#: 967a7c71f0f0436486848380bb4a2ca7
msgid "If you're getting a lot of electrical noise on your sensor (from you rover's motors or the other electronics) you can improve performance markedly by adding a simple filter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:249
#: 91da4e7fd29440feb2b2c93822a1e740
msgid "To avoid spikes in the sonar data you should connect with a shielded cable and include a cap and resistor to filter the power to the sonar as described in \\ `this tutorial <http://www.diydrones.com/profiles/blogs/fantastic-maxbotics-tutorial-on-using-sonor-sensors-with-multicop>`__ on techniques to reduce noise and improve sensor performance. Maxbotics sells the necessary components `here <http://www.maxbotix.com/Ultrasonic_Sensors/MB7961.htm>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/sonar-sensors.rst:259
#: acf44afbbad64a40b1599e45ccef183c
msgid "This image shows Slash Rover's twin sonars and shielded cables as part of a power filtering setup."
msgstr ""
